 "notes": "I read 40 stations, numbered 1–40, from lines 3267–3307. None has a keeper's name, a mark or a light character.\n\n**How the fields were filled**\n- **Fragments** are copied from each line with the leading indent removed. Spacing inside the line is kept.\n- **Other** holds the five data columns in printed order (birds striking lantern / daily entries / species in entries / height / miles from shore), with their original spacing. It also holds the printed note instead of numbers: 'No reply.', 'Do.' or 'Destroyed in storm, Nov. 1881.'.\n- **Names** keep the comma printed straight after them (e.g. 'Fastnet,', 'Oyster Island, N.,'), following your example 'Farn, Inner,'. For the three light-ships the comma comes after the type, so the name has no comma and the type is 'Lt.-ship'.\n- **Place** is given as printed, so most rows show 'do.' (ditto = the county last named). Wicklow Head (no. 9) prints 'do' with no full stop. The counties these stand for: Galley Head, Old Head, Kinsale = Co. Cork; Barrels Rock, Tuskar = Wexford; Wicklow Head = Wicklow; Howth Baily, Rockabill = Dublin; Rathlin = Antrim; Dunree Head through Killybegs = Donegal; Eagle Island E./W. through Clare Island = Mayo; Slyne Head S., the Arran Islands and Straw Island = Galway; Tearaght through Skelligs = Kerry.\n- **Coast** is null for every row. The list has no sub-headings; it sits under the section heading 'IRISH COAST: AUTUMN.', which is the obvious value if you want one.\n\n**Rows to know about**\n- **No. 7 Tuskar** spans two lines. 'large' is on line 3273 and 'numbers' starts line 3274 in the birds-striking column. I joined them in other as 'large numbers     12 …'.\n- **No. 27 Blackrock** gives 'Numbers' rather than a count in the birds-striking column.\n- **No. 6 Barrels Rock** has '?' in the height column.\n- **No. 1 Fastnet** is the only row whose height says 'ft.'.\n- **No-reply rows:** 'Do.' at nos. 36–37 repeats 'No reply.' from no. 35. Nos. 9, 14, 18, 22, 28, 33, 35, 36 and 37 sent no return.\n- **No. 40 Calf Rock** has 'Destroyed in storm, Nov. 1881.' instead of data.\n- **Dashes:** nos. 17, 25, 26, 38 and 39 show '--' for birds, entries and species but give a height and/or distance. They did not say 'No reply.', so I can't tell from this table whether they sent an empty return or none.\n\n**Spellings and types kept as printed**\n- 'Innishtrahull' (usually Inishtrahull), 'Arran Island N./S.' and 'Arranmore' (Aran, Aranmore), 'Howth Baily', 'Valentia' (the usual 19th-century spelling).\n- 'Oyster Island, N.' has a comma before 'N.', unlike 'Eagle Island E.' and 'Slyne Head N.'.\n- Barrels Rock (no. 6) has no 'Lt.-ship' in its row, although Barrels was a light-vessel station. Only Coningbeg, Arklow S. and Kish Bank carry 'Lt.-ship'.\n- 'Old Head, Kinsale,' is kept as one name (Old Head of Kinsale), not split into name and place.",
